#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078245 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "982052d0-32dc-557a-b501-cfb011787f22" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/id #uuid "63a9a3bd-b7ce-5c1d-a138-0bc8f8f87ce6" :question/math-academy-id "e-536" :question/problem "For the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=2,\n$$\n\nuse the tangent line approximation at $x=1$ to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1) = 2.$ We also know that $y'(x) = 2xy,$ so\n\n$$\ny'(1) = 2(1)(2) = 4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and $y'(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+(4)(x-1) \\\\  & =2+4x-4 \\\\  & =4x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)$ using $L\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right).$ We get\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac 3 2 \\right)\\approx L\\left(\\dfrac 3 2 \\right) = 4\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right) - 2 = 4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078287 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e731217-ca61-5295-aeb5-0c3febd250d8" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the First Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/id #uuid "45166435-4bf0-5356-baa3-436258817620" :question/math-academy-id "e-7700" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' =7x-2y, \\qquad y(1)=-4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{10}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | $\\, ? \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=-4,$ we fill $y=-4$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =7x-2y \\\\  & =7\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ | $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078298 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0eb0c7f2-a559-5324-8778-ca5cab083864" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/id #uuid "53e498a9-cb99-57bf-9b24-3cf67dc9a021" :question/math-academy-id "e-9340" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' =2y^2-x^2, \\qquad y(1)=3\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(2).$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2-1 = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =18-1 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =17\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =3+17 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n| $2$ | $20$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx 20.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "982052d0-32dc-557a-b501-cfb011787f22" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2f9918d-fa1a-5c07-9371-485af0feb143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296617} {:answer/id #uuid "9075fece-7dbe-5985-b1ba-332ab1c9dc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9075fece-7dbe-5985-b1ba-332ab1c9dc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296618} :answer-field/id #uuid "20c9fdc7-199f-5aa1-9f1c-b95b30274e80" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8be07587-b2cb-55a6-8ee8-65d4e7dd1f00" :question/math-academy-id "q-347888" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x-1),\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=0$ to approximate $y(1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(1)≈$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(0)=2.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=y(x-1),$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=2(0-1)=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0) \\\\  & =2+(-2)(x-0) \\\\  & =2-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(1)$ using $L(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ≈L(1) \\\\  & =2-2(1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "982052d0-32dc-557a-b501-cfb011787f22" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9a069c9-05f4-5962-8e4f-990a822bdf90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9a069c9-05f4-5962-8e4f-990a822bdf90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296625} :answer-field/id #uuid "649fe6d2-57d0-521b-9de9-0eb0402e3951" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c92129c-0038-56dd-8216-6aeaf961bef6" :question/math-academy-id "q-353287" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}},\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{4}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(\\frac{3}{4})≈$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{1}{2}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{4}.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and ${y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+(-1)(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(\\frac{3}{4})$ using $L(\\frac{3}{4}).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{3}{4}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "982052d0-32dc-557a-b501-cfb011787f22" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86d807f1-250e-5b59-877a-f176af14fc98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077332} {:answer/id #uuid "d5bdcd7b-18bc-5d6d-90c8-d4fd1f7dfc4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077333} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc123d8-ea2a-5586-ba53-d0a92df3de2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077334} {:answer/id #uuid "36f28bbd-df7a-5487-be76-91e2e112eb84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077335} {:answer/id #uuid "42342e51-3f27-55c3-8c38-3bfd87de6094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86d807f1-250e-5b59-877a-f176af14fc98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077332} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf304bc2-9faa-57b2-9e27-4add9f248d13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077331}] :question/id #uuid "0ef00d9b-37f3-552d-9152-aa4571428125" :question/math-academy-id "q-53846" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$y^{′} = \\frac{1}{x}, y(1) = 2$.\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x = 1$ to approximate $y(1.5)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e731217-ca61-5295-aeb5-0c3febd250d8" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the First Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c61657e-3c9f-5f80-8aab-3edd2edec82b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077980} {:answer/id #uuid "b225320c-8243-50fa-910c-c9d282796be2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077981} {:answer/id #uuid "dfad3c17-74d8-5998-8cf1-7b67f1f1ba51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077982} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9d61fe-8026-51b0-9988-03557d908977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077983} {:answer/id #uuid "44bf39e8-0b5c-51b9-9b38-caa26ea57b78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c61657e-3c9f-5f80-8aab-3edd2edec82b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077980} :answer-field/id #uuid "8169b51c-9eb7-51db-a72b-3ed366295dd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077979}] :question/id #uuid "6bed8567-9551-54dd-9ce0-a955a0e1290b" :question/math-academy-id "q-86900" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y^{′} = 3x - 4y, y(1) = 2$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{2}$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ |  |  | $$? |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0eb0c7f2-a559-5324-8778-ca5cab083864" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "801f51e4-3613-517f-b6de-a515ccbfb99b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077998} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d8c312-d956-5e39-b6a9-429408c3b470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077999} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2f5605-88a8-5719-b54e-9b1039e613e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078000} {:answer/id #uuid "734505f0-66ed-5cbd-9d9b-444df43fe413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078001} {:answer/id #uuid "738a4ec3-6076-53f5-86a2-723f3e91ebb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "801f51e4-3613-517f-b6de-a515ccbfb99b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077998} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa12230c-743f-59a1-a9f5-336ee5be0818" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-f